From 3f45a1e4d2f9b2b46b2f0b0b0b0b0b0b0b0b0b0b0b0b0b0b0b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: noname <noname@example.com>
Date: Wed, 4 Nov 2026 1:30:00 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] 0040 keep the stream window's capture owner armed, and say who
 turns it off

`0039` closed one half of the input gap and opened another. The r0.66 attempt
(`play-d2b-2.log`, the window launched Destiny 2 by itself through
`--launch-app-id=100362311`) answers the question it was built for in one line,
and the answer is neither of the two theories the series had left open:

  NVST embedded input tally: active=false pushed=1 dropped=48 keys=48 moves=0
      absolutes=1 buttons=0 other=0 queue=0x295638d0
  NVST native input drain: available=true clearedPolls=3272 keys=0 absolutes=1
      moves=0 buttons=0 queue=0x295638d0

Both sides name the **same** queue, so there is no second queue nobody reads. The
seat's one absolute went in while the gate was open (`pushed=1 absolutes=1`, and
the drain forwarded it: `NVST native input rx: events=[mouse-absolute(5)]`,
`NVST encoded input tx: route=ControlPartial`), and the window's 24 Enter presses
of the opening clock, 48 key transitions, were refused by `EmbeddedInputCapture::submit`
(`embedded_input.rs:139: if self.active.load(Ordering::Acquire)`) because the
queue's owner had gone inactive. The window's side says it armed capture once and
never touched it again:

  01:16:07.211 wayland-video input: streamer capture requested=0 inputAllowed=1
  01:16:07.355 wayland-video input: streamer capture requested=1 inputAllowed=1
  01:16:07.356 wayland-video input: capture active
  (nothing else the whole session)

So the gate went off with no app-side line, and `submit` cannot say so either: its
status is `OPENNOW_STREAMER_OK` whether it took the event or dropped it. The code
names the caller. `EmbeddedInputCapture::set_active` has one production entry
point, `opennow_streamer_set_capture_active` (`crates/opennow-streamer-ffi/src/lib.rs:1643`),
and the native stream window reaches it through `setVideoInputCapture`, which logs.
The application's own Qt video item reaches the same owner without logging anything:
`StreamVideoItem::syncCaptureState` (`src/streaming/StreamVideoItemInput.cpp:956`)
calls `s_nativeRuntime->setCaptureActive(desired, …)` directly, and `desired` is
false for an item that has no active Qt window focus - which is every run on this
board, because the stream window is weston's own window and not a `QQuickWindow`.
The app's Qt item switches the capture owner off a moment after the native stream
window switched it on, and nothing switches it back: `WaylandVideoSurface::setCaptureActive`
returns early while its own `m_captureActive` still says active, which is exactly
what makes the presses silent. This is also the first time the series has a session
where the presses never reached the host at all, and it is why the `0032`/`0033`
clock never closed Steam's opening notice on this board: the notice was answered
by nothing.

Two halves, both greppable:

1. The streamer says when the owner changes state, so the next reader does not
   have to read the code to find the caller. `embedded_input.rs` prints, on the
   gate's first call and on every call that changes the state it leaves behind:

     NVST embedded capture gate: call={} active={} previous={} relative={}
         window_handle={} raw={} queue={:p}

   which names the state, the arguments the caller passed, and the queue - the
   same address the tally and the drain print.

2. The stream window re-applies the state it believes it has, because the owner
   is shared and the other owner says nothing. `WaylandVideoSurface` gains
   `reassertCaptureOwner(reason)`, which goes through the existing
   `refreshCaptureState` and only while the window's own `m_captureActive` says
   active, so a keyboard that left the window or a teardown still owns the
   decision and this cannot undo it. It runs before every key transition the
   window submits - `submitKey`, and the opening clock's press and release - and
   once a second while the window is live, which is what keeps a player's pointer
   and button alive across the switch as well. The line is
   `wayland-video input: capture re-armed: %s`, next to the existing
   `wayland-video input: streamer capture requested=%d inputAllowed=%d`.

The gate log counts calls, not bytes, and the re-arm costs one call to the streamer
per press plus one a second: nothing is buffered, no rate is added, and the window
that never had capture armed is unchanged.

Upstream-Status: Inappropriate [STM32MP25 performance work]

--- a/native/opennow-streamer/crates/opennow-streamer-platform/src/embedded_input.rs
+++ b/native/opennow-streamer/crates/opennow-streamer-platform/src/embedded_input.rs
@@ -74,6 +74,43 @@
     }
 }
 
+// The gate above is silent about the side that owns it: `submit` answers the
+// embedder with `OpenNowStreamerStatus::Ok` whether it took the event or threw
+// it away, so the app's own log says the window pressed and nothing else says
+// the press died at the gate. The tally names the queue and counts what crossed
+// it, but the sessions so far read it as one fact: the window armed capture once
+// (`wayland-video input: streamer capture requested=1 inputAllowed=1` beside
+// `capture active`) and the tally then said `active=false dropped=48`, with no
+// second capture call anywhere in the app's log. Something turned the owner off
+// without saying so, and only the streamer's own code can say when that happens,
+// so this prints the state change itself: the gate's first call, and every call
+// that changes the state it leaves behind.
+
+fn embedded_capture_gate_log(
+    active: bool,
+    previous: bool,
+    relative_mouse: bool,
+    window_handle: usize,
+    raw: bool,
+    queue: Arc<CapturedInputQueue>,
+) {
+    static CALLS: AtomicUsize = AtomicUsize::new(0);
+
+    let call = CALLS.fetch_add(1, Ordering::Relaxed) + 1;
+    if call == 1 || active != previous {
+        eprintln!(
+            "NVST embedded capture gate: call={} active={} previous={} relative={} window_handle={} raw={} queue={:p}",
+            call,
+            active,
+            previous,
+            relative_mouse,
+            window_handle,
+            raw,
+            queue,
+        );
+    }
+}
+
 #[derive(Debug, Clone, Copy, PartialEq, Eq)]
 pub enum EmbeddedLocalAction {
     Guide,
@@ -175,6 +212,7 @@
     }
 
     pub fn set_active(&self, active: bool, relative_mouse: bool, window_handle: usize) -> bool {
+        let previous = self.active.load(Ordering::Acquire);
         let mut gamepads = self
             .gamepads
             .lock()
@@ -203,6 +241,14 @@
         #[cfg(not(target_os = "windows"))]
         let settings = MouseSettings::UNACCELERATED;
         self.queue.set_mouse_settings(settings);
+        embedded_capture_gate_log(
+            active,
+            previous,
+            relative_mouse,
+            window_handle,
+            raw_relative,
+            Arc::clone(&self.queue),
+        );
         raw_relative
     }
 
--- a/opennow-qt/src/streaming/rendering/WaylandVideoSurface.cpp
+++ b/opennow-qt/src/streaming/rendering/WaylandVideoSurface.cpp
@@ -264,6 +264,25 @@
     if (!active) releaseHeldKeys();
     if (qEnvironmentVariableIsSet("OPENNOW_LOG_INPUT"))
         qInfo("wayland-video input: capture %s", active ? "active" : "inactive");
+    // The capture owner is shared with the application's Qt video item, which
+    // switches it off on its own clock and never tells this window (see the
+    // header's note), so the state this window asked for has to be re-applied.
+    // Once a second is enough: it costs one call to the streamer and it keeps the
+    // pointer and the button alive as well as the keys, which re-apply it
+    // themselves at the press. The clock is stopped when the window goes away, so
+    // a session that ended cannot re-arm capture into the interface.
+    if (active && !m_rearmTimer) {
+        m_rearmTimer = new QTimer(this);
+        m_rearmTimer->setTimerType(Qt::PreciseTimer);
+        m_rearmTimer->setInterval(1000);
+        connect(m_rearmTimer, &QTimer::timeout, this, [this] { reassertCaptureOwner("clock"); });
+    }
+    if (m_rearmTimer) {
+        if (active)
+            m_rearmTimer->start();
+        else
+            m_rearmTimer->stop();
+    }
 }
 
 void WaylandVideoSurface::refreshCaptureState()
@@ -271,6 +290,17 @@
     if (m_input.capture) m_input.capture(m_captureActive, m_serverCursorHidden);
 }
 
+void WaylandVideoSurface::reassertCaptureOwner(const char *reason)
+{
+    // Only the window's own decision counts: a keyboard that left the window or
+    // a teardown that stopped the window already said inactive, and this must not
+    // undo that.
+    if (!m_captureActive || !m_input.capture) return;
+    refreshCaptureState();
+    if (qEnvironmentVariableIsSet("OPENNOW_LOG_INPUT"))
+        qInfo("wayland-video input: capture re-armed: %s", reason);
+}
+
 void WaylandVideoSurface::setServerCursorHidden(bool hidden)
 {
     if (m_serverCursorHidden == hidden) return;
@@ -535,6 +565,11 @@
         return;
     }
     const quint16 modifiers = wireModifiers(virtualKey);
+    // The press only reaches the host while the capture owner is armed, and the
+    // owner is shared with the application's Qt video item, which switches it off
+    // without telling this window; re-apply the state just before the press so
+    // the key the player typed is not silently dropped at the owner's gate.
+    reassertCaptureOwner("key");
     m_input.key(virtualKey, modifiers, pressed);
     if (qEnvironmentVariableIsSet("OPENNOW_LOG_INPUT"))
         qInfo("wayland-video input: key %u vk=0x%x mods=0x%x pressed=%d", evdev, virtualKey,
@@ -604,6 +639,7 @@
         // on the 5 s spacing the follow-up answers keep.
         const int held = m_enterHeldMs;
         m_enterHeldMs = 0;
+        reassertCaptureOwner("enter release");
         if (m_input.key) m_input.key(0x0d, 0, false);
         if (qEnvironmentVariableIsSet("OPENNOW_LOG_INPUT"))
             qInfo("wayland-video input: opening enter held for %d ms and released", held);
@@ -622,6 +658,7 @@
         --m_enterLeft;
         // Enter is VK_RETURN; the press goes out now and the release follows on
         // the next round, holdMs later.
+        reassertCaptureOwner("enter press");
         m_input.key(0x0d, 0, true);
         m_enterHeldMs = holdMs;
         qInfo("wayland-video input: opening enter answered (%d left)", m_enterLeft);
--- a/opennow-qt/src/streaming/rendering/WaylandVideoSurface.h
+++ b/opennow-qt/src/streaming/rendering/WaylandVideoSurface.h
@@ -103,6 +103,19 @@
     // owner to re-apply the current state when that changes.
     void refreshCaptureState();
 
+    // The stream window is not the only thing that owns the streamer's capture
+    // gate. The application's own Qt video item asks the same owner to switch
+    // itself off, and it reaches the runtime straight (StreamVideoItem::syncCaptureState
+    // calls NativeStreamRuntime::setCaptureActive with no log line of its own), so
+    // the gate this window opened goes inactive a moment later and every press
+    // made after that is dropped inside EmbeddedInputCapture::submit - the submit
+    // still answers OPENNOW_STREAMER_OK, so the window's own log looks clean. The
+    // window therefore re-asserts the state it believes it has, before each key it
+    // submits and once a second while it is live, which keeps the pointer and the
+    // button a player makes alive as well. Deciding active or inactive stays this
+    // class's job: nothing here re-arms a keyboard that left the window.
+    void reassertCaptureOwner(const char *reason);
+
     // The video advances on a clock of its own instead of once per interface
     // repaint. The callback is called from the interface thread and is expected
     // to pull one frame from the decoder and present it here.
@@ -248,6 +261,9 @@
     QTimer *m_enterTimer = nullptr;
     int m_enterLeft = 0;
     int m_enterHeldMs = 0;
+    // The clock that keeps the capture state this window asked for applied
+    // against the owner's own switches (see reassertCaptureOwner).
+    QTimer *m_rearmTimer = nullptr;
     quint32 m_xkbModifiers = 0;
     bool m_pointerInside = false;
 
